270
00:10:55,672 --> 00:10:58,317
was the tail previously. It's just not tied

271
00:10:58,317 --> 00:11:00,544
to the tail property anymore. That's why

272
00:11:00,544 --> 00:11:02,804
this order here matters because here we

273
00:11:02,804 --> 00:11:05,304
still reach out to the old tail and update

274
00:11:05,448 --> 00:11:07,291
the old tail's next property.

275
00:11:08,077 --> 00:11:11,154
And thereafter, we replace the old tail in

276
00:11:11,202 --> 00:11:13,269
the linked list with our new node.

277
00:11:14,169 --> 00:11:15,989
Now the element which was our tail

278
00:11:16,183 --> 00:11:18,695
previously still exists, and the next

279
00:11:18,695 --> 00:11:21,659
property of that previous tail element also

280
00:11:21,659 --> 00:11:23,788
still points at the new node. So that is not

281
00:11:23,871 --> 00:11:25,020
overwritten by this line.

282
00:11:26,950 --> 00:11:28,633
Now of course, this implementation has one

283
00:11:28,681 --> 00:11:31,401
flaw. Initially tail is null, so reaching

284
00:11:31,498 --> 00:11:33,651
out to the next property will fail.

285
00:11:34,230 --> 00:11:37,137
So we should actually check if we have a

286
00:11:37,268 --> 00:11:37,545
tail

287
00:11:38,101 --> 00:11:40,004
before we try to do so

288
00:11:40,795 --> 00:11:44,183
before we try to edit our old tail. If we

289
00:11:44,264 --> 00:11:45,345
don't have a tail,

290
00:11:45,894 --> 00:11:47,943
so basically if our list is empty,

291
00:11:48,524 --> 00:11:50,751
then of course we don't want to update the

292
00:11:50,880 --> 00:11:52,526
tail because then there is nothing to

293
00:11:52,558 --> 00:11:55,156
update. So if we don't have a tail yet, it's

294
00:11:55,156 --> 00:11:57,625
enough to just set the tail to the new node.

295
00:11:58,061 --> 00:12:00,134
We always wanna do that, but we only wanna

296
00:12:00,214 --> 00:12:02,207
update the old tail if we have one.

297
00:12:04,006 --> 00:12:07,203
We also of course want to set our head equal

298
00:12:07,235 --> 00:12:09,089
to the new node if we don't have a head yet.

299
00:12:09,902 --> 00:12:11,475
Which would be the case if the list is still

300
00:12:11,588 --> 00:12:11,845
empty.

301
00:12:12,567 --> 00:12:14,782
So I also want to add another if check and

302
00:12:14,879 --> 00:12:16,869
check if we have a head property.

303
00:12:17,527 --> 00:12:19,277
And if we don't have it, hence the

304
00:12:19,277 --> 00:12:20,947
exclamation mark, if head is

305
00:12:21,781 --> 00:12:24,430
falsy, so if it's null for example, then I

306
00:12:24,494 --> 00:12:26,517
want to set this head equal to the new node

307
00:12:26,581 --> 00:12:26,806
as well.

308
00:12:27,641 --> 00:12:28,171
Typically

309
00:12:28,765 --> 00:12:31,429
when we append a value, the head should not

310
00:12:31,494 --> 00:12:33,645
be updated because the head is the first

311
00:12:33,661 --> 00:12:35,828
value of the list and appending means that

312
00:12:35,828 --> 00:12:37,850
we add it at the end. But if the list is

313
00:12:37,978 --> 00:12:40,047
empty, if it has no element yet, if both

314
00:12:40,223 --> 00:12:42,484
head and tail is null, then of course the

315
00:12:42,484 --> 00:12:44,649
head should also be updated because then we

316
00:12:44,649 --> 00:12:47,440
just added the only new element to this

317
00:12:47,440 --> 00:12:49,492
list. So now the list has exactly one

318
00:12:49,621 --> 00:12:52,042
element, and of course that element then, if

319
00:12:52,042 --> 00:12:54,111
it's the only element, is both head and

320
00:12:54,111 --> 00:12:56,164
tail. So this is kind of a special case.

321
00:12:56,901 --> 00:12:59,243
So this is now an append function that

322
00:12:59,243 --> 00:13:00,509
should do the trick.

323
00:13:01,728 --> 00:13:04,605
Now in order to see Whether our linked list

324
00:13:04,686 --> 00:13:07,360
and its append function works, we also

325
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:09,357
certainly want some method in our linked

326
00:13:09,357 --> 00:13:12,112
list that outputs all the nodes so that we

327
00:13:12,112 --> 00:13:14,480
can print it to the console, for example.

328
00:13:15,011 --> 00:13:16,461
That's something we're going to implement

329
00:13:16,526 --> 00:13:16,864
next.
